== Figure of Merit
The figure of Merit (FOM) is a parameter that is used to evaluate the performance of the microphone. More charge is generate, the more sensitivity the microphone has. //take reference to one journal article.
Also the flatness of the electricity response given by the microphone is another important parameter, avoiding the potential distortion of the sound. For these sound frequency that has low response in the electrical field, the microphone will have a low sensitivity. To compensate for this, the external circuit should amplify these low-response frequency, leading to amplication of the noise. Therefore, the total harmonic distortion (THD) increases. //有待考究
=== Total stress on PEM<stress>
Since the pizeoelectricity is a linear relationship with the stress applied on the PEM, we can focus on the total stress that given by the sound wave. The total stress can be written as @total-stress,
#mitex(`
  $T_j = \iint_{\mathcal{S}} F_j \, dA$
`)<total-stress>
supposed $j$ is the direction of the stress, $F_j$ is the force applied on the PEM at every position, and $A$ is area of the PEM. The total stress is the integration of the force applied on the PEM. The more stress given by the sound wave, the more charge will be generated.
=== Total charge generated by PEM<charge>
To direct evaluate the performance, we can directly use total charge. The total charge generated can be written as @total-charge, 
#mitex(`
  $Q = \iint_{\mathcal{S}} \sigma \, dA$
`)<total-charge>
while the $sigma$ is the charge density on the PEM. The total charge is the integration of the charge density on PEM. 
=== Across over the frequency range
In this project, we only consider the frequency could be heard by human, which is from 20~Hz -- 20~kHz. The values from @stress and @charge need to be integral over the frequency range. The FOM can be written as @FOM or @FOM2, 
$ "FOM"=integral_(20)^(20000) Q_p d f $<FOM>
or 
$ "FOM"=integral_(20)^(20000) T_p d f $<FOM2>
where $p$ is the index of the frequency. 

